      \field{labeltitlesource}{title}
      \field{abstract}{Passive Acoustic Monitoring (PAM) enables non-invasive, large-scale assessment of animal biodiversity, but the resulting audio archives routinely exceed what domain experts can annotate manually. Existing annotation tools address parts of this challenge through spectrogram visualisation, automated inference, or collaborative review, but none support the full annotation cycle needed to produce high-quality, interoperable datasets at scale. We present YAPAT (Yet Another PAM Annotation Tool), a web-based platform for interactive machine learning applied to the curation of annotated PAM datasets. YAPAT integrates four capabilities that, in combination, are absent from prior tools: (i) ontology-grounded label spaces that link annotations to authoritative biological and environmental taxonomies (GBIF, ENVO), with explicit local concept support, supporting dataset interoperability and reuse; (ii) multi-label active learning that ranks unlabelled snippets by a composite informativeness score, reducing the expert effort required to produce a well-labelled dataset; (iii) two complementary cold-start mechanisms: upload-based vector similarity search over frozen BirdNET embeddings, enabling annotation before any classifier exists; and weakly supervised sound event detection via multiple instance learning, enabling classifier initialisation from sound collections labelled at the file level; and (iv) interactive 2D projection of BirdNET embeddings for exploratory annotation. The system is collaborative, browser-based, and open source, and produces reusable, ontology-grounded annotation datasets suitable for deposition and downstream analysis.}
